>> title: Shawarma Spice
>> category: Spice Blends & Marinades
>> tags: spice blend, middle eastern, sumac, allspice, toasting
>> counters: Shawarma Counter
>> aka: shawarma seasoning, shawarma rub, shwarma spice, shawerma spice, chawarma spice, doner spice, döner spice, gyro seasoning, seven spice, baharat
>> pairs-with: zaatar, dukkah
>> servings: 16
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: narrow — three minutes on the whole spices, then the pan comes off before the ground ones go in; it is rubbed onto meat rather than cooked into a sauce, so a scorched batch is tasted directly and there is nothing else in the dish to cover it
>> step.1: toast whole spices 3 min
>> step.2: grind fine
>> step.3: stir in the ground spices
>> step.4: cool 10 min, then jar

Toast @coriander seed{2%Tbs}(12 g), @cumin seed{2%Tbs}(12 g), @black peppercorns{1%Tbs}(7 g), @allspice berries{2%tsp}(5 g), @green cardamom pods{8}(cracked) and @whole cloves{6} in a dry #skillet{} over medium heat ~toast{3%min}, shaking the pan the whole time, until the cumin darkens a shade and you can smell it across the room. This is the blend under the stack, and it is the reason a home shawarma tastes almost right and not right — every shop has one, none of them writes it down, and it has no cookbook name to look up.

Grind @&(~1)toasted spices{} with @cinnamon stick{1}(broken up) in a #spice grinder{} until nothing grits between your fingers.

Stir @ground sumac{1%Tbs}(7 g), @sweet paprika{1%Tbs}(7 g), @ground turmeric{1%tsp}(3 g), @cayenne{1/2%tsp} and @kosher salt{1%Tbs}(15 g) into @&(~1)ground spice{}. The sumac goes in after the heat and never into the pan — toasted sumac loses the sourness that is the only reason it is in here.

Cool @&(~1)blend{} ~cool{10%min} on the counter before it goes into a jar, or the trapped steam cakes it solid. It holds its edge about three months.
